Hello! I am so glad you reached out. It sounds like your questions involve planning for the future, protecting your family, or understanding what happens after service—very common concerns for new soldiers and their families. Whether you are on active duty in an army unit, a reserve officer, looking to extend your coverage later, or want to understand how the VA can help, I am here to provide a warm, complete answer based on official documents.

### **1. How do I qualify for SGLI while I'm still serving?**
You are eligible to receive full-time Servicemembers' Group Life Insurance (SGLI) if any one of the following conditions is true [p946]:
*   You are currently active duty in the Army, Navy, Air Force, Marines, or Coast Guard;
*   You are a commissioned member of the NOAA or USPHS (Public Health Service);
*   You are a cadet or midshipman enrolled at a U.S. military academy;
*   You are a ROTC cadet or midshipman who has been engaged in authorized training and practice cruises;
*   You are a member of the Ready Reserve or National Guard assigned to such units and scheduled to perform at least 12 periods of inactive training per year;
*   You are a volunteer in an Individual Ready Reserve (IRR) mobilization category.

