Based on **In-transit vehicle permits (temporary registrations)**, various types of papers qualify as acceptable proof of insurance for driving while moving within New York State if you are not a resident, including NY State Insurance ID Cards (FS-20 or FS-21), temporary IDs (FS-75), and certificates for hire passenger vehicles [p2315]. These documents must be prepared no more than seven days after the application date and bear specific markings like "in-transit" or "in-transit permit," though they cannot be accepted if a barcode reader on your vehicle cannot scan them [p2313]. Alternatively, you can also provide an insurance binder from an out-of-state agent, which requires detailed information about the company, insured, policy details, and vehicle description [p2313].

Regarding specific types like those referenced in **New York State Insurance Requirements** (which appear on pages 5264 or elsewhere depending on your version but refer to standard documents), a temporary registration is also acceptable if prepared within seven days of issuance. For out-of-state vehicles not registered here, the only valid form is the NY State Insurance ID Card specifically required for those situations [p1701].

**Important Note**: You cannot obtain out-of-state insurance while retaining your New York vehicle registration because doing so would result in a lapse of coverage and potential suspension of your license. If you move out of state, you must surrender your plates immediately;
